@page "/sidebar"

<PageContainer Title="TwSidebar" Path="/sidebar" Description="TwSidebar is a Blazor sidebar for navigation. Build it from SidebarContent markup or data-driven NavigationItems, with IsSearchable and a collapsible layout." ThemeType="typeof(TwSidebarTheme)">
    <PageCard Title="Basic sidebar">
        <Description>
            <code>TwSidebar</code> has all the functionality you'd want in a single component for a sidebar. You can customise the sidebar using both tailwind classes and additional TwBlazor Theme styling.
        </Description>
        <iframe src="/sidebar/preview" title="TwSidebar basic example preview" class="w-full h-50 border-0 rounded-lg"></iframe>
        <ExampleCodeBlock Razor="@CodeExamples.Get("SidebarBasic")" />
    </PageCard>

    <PageCard Title="Data-driven navigation (programmatic)">
        <Description>
            Instead of hand-writing <code>SidebarContent</code> markup, pass a <code>List&lt;NavigationItem&gt;</code> to <code>NavigationItems</code> and TwSidebar renders the links, nested groups, and (with <code>IsSearchable</code>) a search box for you - this is exactly how this documentation site's own sidebar is built, see <code>Navigation.razor.cs</code>.
        </Description>
        <Description>
            Set <code>Icon</code> on a <code>NavigationItem</code> to render a <code>TwIcon</code> before its label. It works on links and parent items at any depth. The icon is decorative and hidden from screen readers, so the label stays the item's name. It is styled by <code>NavigationItemIcon</code> on <code>TwSidebarTheme</code>.
        </Description>
        <iframe src="/sidebar/preview-navigation" title="TwSidebar data-driven navigation example preview" class="w-full h-50 border-0 rounded-lg"></iframe>
        <ExampleCodeBlock Razor="@CodeExamples.Get("SidebarNavigationItems")" CSharp="@CodeExamples.Get("SidebarNavigationItemsCode")" />
    </PageCard>

    <PageCard Title="Item content">
        <Description>
            Use <code>NavigationItemContent</code> to render your own content after the label of the items generated from <code>NavigationItems</code>, such as a badge or a count. The template is called for every item and receives the <code>NavigationItem</code> as its context, so render nothing for the items that need no extra content. This documentation site uses it for the "New" badges in its own sidebar.
        </Description>
        <Description>
            It works on parent items as well as links: on a parent item the content sits between the label and the chevron, as on "Content" below. A badge on a parent can stand for everything inside it, in place of a badge on every child.
        </Description>
        <Description>
            Identify items by their <code>Id</code> or <code>Href</code>, not by reference, because the template receives a copy of the item while the sidebar is being searched. The content is placed inside the item's link or toggle button, so keep it non-interactive; any text in it is read out as part of the item's name. The wrapper around the content is styled by <code>NavigationItemContent</code> on <code>TwSidebarTheme</code>.
        </Description>
        <iframe src="/sidebar/preview-item-content" title="TwSidebar item content example preview" class="w-full h-50 border-0 rounded-lg"></iframe>
        <ExampleCodeBlock Razor="@CodeExamples.Get("SidebarItemContent")" CSharp="@CodeExamples.Get("SidebarItemContentCode")" />
    </PageCard>
</PageContainer>